{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head 4db9a50c6a07346ebdd09ea7e38ac27f29140df9, signing both artifacts: cmd/chess/main.go 14794865 and cmd/chess/main_test.go ad3155ce. Filed by hand rather than through gs review because the MCP has no review tool — that is request 27f88d84 in the core workroom, still unlanded, and this verdict is one more instance of the papercut it names.\n\nI ran the behaviour rather than reading the diff, because I wrote the request this head answers and the failure mode I most needed to avoid was approving my own prescription.\n\nCONDITION 1, exact head. Detached worktree at 4db9a50c, clean tree, branch request/chess-serve-startup-check present on origin. It contains current main rather than amending the approved first-user head — the sequencing my request required is honoured: b9483eaa merged as 5c29123 and this sits on top of it.\n\nCONDITION 2, refusal before listening. Three cases, each run against the built binary. Absent repository: exit 1, empty stdout, stderr \"open chess repository \"/nonexistent/nope\": resolve git dirs: ... cannot change to\". A plain non-chess git repository: exit 1, empty stdout, path and reason on stderr. An unreadable repository, chmod 000: exit 1, same shape. In every case no URL reached stdout.\n\nCONDITION 3, the wired regression. The test drives the real command through an os.Args[0] subprocess helper and asserts all four things: non-zero exit, empty stdout, stderr naming both the repository path and the reason, and the address rebindable afterwards. I confirmed rebindability independently by binding the port myself after a refusal. The test also carries a three-second context timeout, so the pre-fix behaviour is reported as a failure rather than hanging the suite — a detail I did not ask for and would have missed.\n\nThen I mutated it, which is the only thing that proves a test. Deleting the three-line guard makes TestServeCommandRejectsMissingRepositoryBeforeListening fail, and it fails by reproducing the original defect exactly: stdout \"http://127.0.0.1:58136\", stderr empty. The test is pinned to the behaviour, not to the implementation. main.go restored byte-identical afterwards, worktree clean.\n\nCONDITION 4, runtime behaviour preserved, which is the one a startup check most easily breaks. I initialised a real chess repository, served it, confirmed the UI renders, then deleted the repository underneath the running process. The server stayed alive and answered HTTP 503 \"repository is unavailable\". No crash, no change to the per-request path. The addition is genuinely additive.\n\nCONDITION 5, gates. gofmt silent, go build zero, go vet zero, full suite -count=1 green at the exact head: gitseq-chess ok 19.1s, cmd/chess ok 17.2s.\n\nARCHITECTURE: preserved. The change is confined to the cmd/chess outer command surface — three lines in runServe, between the listen-address validation and server construction. It touches no handler, no fold, no web layer, and adds no new dependency: it calls application.OpenProjection, the same entry the read handlers already use. No contract changes, so no reference page needs updating in this head. Both artifacts stand at exact file paths rather than the cmd/chess directory row, which is the shape hugh has just ruled the correct end state.\n\nSECURITY: no defect found, and the direction is fail-closed. The check refuses earlier than before and never later. There is a time-of-check to time-of-use gap by construction — the repository is validated at startup and reopened per request — but it is not a weakness here, because the per-request open remains the authority and I verified it still refuses correctly after the repository is removed. The startup check buys an honest exit code, not an authorization decision, and it would be wrong to make it one. The error text names a path the operator supplied on their own command line and goes to stderr of a local process, so it discloses nothing. The port is not bound before validation, which I confirmed by rebinding it.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut. Three lines reusing an existing call is the smallest fix that closes the defect, and every alternative I considered is worse. One observation offered rather than requested: OpenProjection does real work, so on a very large log this adds its cost to startup before the port opens. That is a latency note, not a defect, and the alternative — validating something cheaper and less truthful — would weaken the guarantee. Discarding both return values is correct rather than wasteful, since the handler must re-read to stay current.\n\nONE THING FOR THE MERGE, not a change request. Live predecessors at these exact paths stand at commits now contained in main — cmd/chess/main.go at 5aab3697 and ba0e06b among them — so the merge must retire them and publish successors at the same two file paths. There is also a directory row ac3b9952 at cmd/chess standing at the merge commit 5c29123; under hugh's ruling that shape is not the end state, so it should not be extended by this landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:14794865dac1f171cbe453b1c454010138fdbc8f","head":"4db9a50c6a07346ebdd09ea7e38ac27f29140df9","verdict":"approved"}}